WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.250
The transpose convolution is

2
00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.860
a key part of the
unit architecture.

3
00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.905
How do you take a
two-by-two inputs

4
00:00:10.905 --> 00:00:14.475
and blow it up into a four-
by-four-dimensional output?

5
00:00:14.475 --> 00:00:17.295
The transpose convolution
lets you do that.

6
00:00:17.295 --> 00:00:19.095
Let's dig into the details.

7
00:00:19.095 --> 00:00:22.280
You're familiar with the
normal convolution in which

8
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.965
a typical layer of
a new network may

9
00:00:24.965 --> 00:00:28.400
input a six by six
by three image,

10
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.490
convolve that with a set of, say,

11
00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:33.965
three by three by three filters

12
00:00:33.965 --> 00:00:35.615
and if you have five of these,

13
00:00:35.615 --> 00:00:37.595
then you end up with an output

14
00:00:37.595 --> 00:00:39.650
that is four by four by five.

15
00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:42.575
A transpose convolution
looks a bit difference.

16
00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:47.630
You might inputs a two-by-two,
said that activation,

17
00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:51.170
convolve that with a
three by three filter,

18
00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.275
and end up with an output
that is four by four,

19
00:00:54.275 --> 00:00:57.355
that's bigger than
the original inputs.

20
00:00:57.355 --> 00:01:00.850
Let's step through a
more detailed example

21
00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:02.320
of how this works.

22
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.230
In this example,
we're going to take

23
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:08.370
a two-by-two inputs like they're

24
00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.050
shown on the left
and we want to end

25
00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.560
up with a four by four outputs.

26
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.665
But to go from two-by-two
to four-by-four,

27
00:01:17.665 --> 00:01:19.240
I'm going to choose to use

28
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.935
a filter that is three by three.

29
00:01:22.935 --> 00:01:25.515
The filter is f by f,

30
00:01:25.515 --> 00:01:28.620
and I'm going to
choose three by three

31
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.815
and let's say that's
the filter we will use.

32
00:01:31.815 --> 00:01:32.925
I'm also going to use

33
00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:38.460
a padding p is equal to
one and in the outputs,

34
00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.645
I'm going to apply,

35
00:01:39.645 --> 00:01:43.460
that's one p padding,
,and then finally,

36
00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.470
the last parameter,
I'm going to use

37
00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:49.515
a stride s equal to
two for this example.

38
00:01:49.515 --> 00:01:52.685
Let's see how the transpose
convolution will work.

39
00:01:52.685 --> 00:01:54.424
In the regular convolution,

40
00:01:54.424 --> 00:01:56.870
you would take the
filter and place it on

41
00:01:56.870 --> 00:02:00.010
top of the inputs and
then multiply and sum up.

42
00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:02.385
In the transpose convolution,

43
00:02:02.385 --> 00:02:05.075
instead of placing the
filter on the input,

44
00:02:05.075 --> 00:02:08.740
you would instead place
a filter on the output.

45
00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:12.005
Let me illustrate that
by mechanically stepping

46
00:02:12.005 --> 00:02:13.700
through the steps of

47
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.350
the transpose
convolution calculation.

48
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.430
Let's starts with
this upper left entry

49
00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:22.225
of the input, which is a two.

50
00:02:22.225 --> 00:02:26.380
We are going to take
this number two and

51
00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:30.340
multiply it by every
single value in

52
00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.290
the filter and we're going

53
00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.510
to take the output
which is three by

54
00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:39.540
three and paste it
in this position.

55
00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.025
Now, the padding area isn't
going to contain any values.

56
00:02:44.025 --> 00:02:46.225
What we're going
to end up doing is

57
00:02:46.225 --> 00:02:50.440
ignore this paddy
region and just throw

58
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:54.820
in four values in the
red highlighted area

59
00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.720
and specifically,

60
00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.755
the upper left entry is zero
times two, so that's zero.

61
00:03:01.755 --> 00:03:07.430
The second entry is one
times two, that is two.

62
00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.230
Down here is two times two,

63
00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:14.180
that's four, and then over here

64
00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.750
is one times two so
that's equal to two.

65
00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.370
Next, let's look at

66
00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.585
the second entry of the
input which is a one.

67
00:03:23.585 --> 00:03:26.060
I'm going to switch to
green pen for this.

68
00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.430
Once again, we're going
to take a one and

69
00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:32.930
multiply by one every single
elements of the filter,

70
00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:36.785
because we're using
a stride of two,

71
00:03:36.785 --> 00:03:40.130
we're now going to
shift to box in which

72
00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:44.895
we copy the numbers
over by two steps.

73
00:03:44.895 --> 00:03:48.515
Again, we'll ignore the area
which is in the padding and

74
00:03:48.515 --> 00:03:51.260
the only area that
we need to copy

75
00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:54.205
the numbers over is
this green shaded area.

76
00:03:54.205 --> 00:03:56.000
You may notice that there is

77
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.475
some overlap between the
places where we copy

78
00:03:58.475 --> 00:04:02.030
the red-colored version
of the filter and

79
00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:05.510
the green version
and we cannot simply

80
00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:09.680
copy the green value
over the red one.

81
00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:13.490
Where the red and the
green boxes overlap,

82
00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:18.325
you add two values together.

83
00:04:18.325 --> 00:04:20.730
Where there's already a

84
00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.060
two from the first
weighted filter,

85
00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.130
you add to it this first

86
00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.010
value from the green
region which is also two.

87
00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.460
You end up with two plus two.

88
00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.780
The next entry, zero
times one is zero,

89
00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.560
then you have one, two
plus zero times one,

90
00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.050
so two plus zero followed by two,

91
00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:44.510
followed by one and again,

92
00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:47.390
we shifted two squares over from

93
00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:49.460
the red box here to the green box

94
00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.390
here because it using
a stride of two.

95
00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:54.225
Next, let's look at

96
00:04:54.225 --> 00:04:58.140
the lower-left entry of
the input, which is three.

97
00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:00.215
We'll take the filter,

98
00:05:00.215 --> 00:05:02.480
multiply every element by three

99
00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:05.685
and we've gone down
by one step here.

100
00:05:05.685 --> 00:05:08.820
We're going to go down
by two steps here.

101
00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:13.625
We will be filling in
our numbers in this

102
00:05:13.625 --> 00:05:16.160
three by three square
and you find that

103
00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.860
the numbers you copying
over are two times three,

104
00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:21.800
which is six, one times three,

105
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:24.350
which is three, zero times three,

106
00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.610
which is zero, and so on,

107
00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:29.850
three, six, three
,and then lastly,

108
00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:35.010
let's go into the last input
element, which is two.

109
00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:38.500
We will multiply every
elements of the filter by

110
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:42.370
two and add them to

111
00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:45.760
this block and you
end up with adding

112
00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:50.190
one times two which is plus two,

113
00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:55.005
and so on for the
rest of the elements.

114
00:05:55.005 --> 00:05:57.940
The final step is to
take all the numbers in

115
00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.295
these four by four matrix of

116
00:06:00.295 --> 00:06:03.025
values in the 16 values
and add them up.

117
00:06:03.025 --> 00:06:04.840
You end up with zero here,

118
00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:09.390
two plus two is four, zero, one,

119
00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:11.340
four plus six is 10,

120
00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:15.344
two plus zero plus three
plus two is seven,

121
00:06:15.344 --> 00:06:17.625
two plus four is six.

122
00:06:17.625 --> 00:06:19.690
I know this looks like a little
bit of a mess because I'm

123
00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:21.850
writing on top of
things there is zero,

124
00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:25.330
three plus four was
seven, zero, two,

125
00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:28.425
six, three plus zero was three,

126
00:06:28.425 --> 00:06:34.395
four, two, hence that's
your four-by-four outputs.

127
00:06:34.395 --> 00:06:36.520
In case you're wondering why

128
00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:38.755
do we have to do it this way,

129
00:06:38.755 --> 00:06:41.575
I think there are
multiple possible ways to

130
00:06:41.575 --> 00:06:45.805
take small inputs and turn
it into bigger outputs,

131
00:06:45.805 --> 00:06:49.900
but the transpose convolution
happens to be one that is

132
00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:52.030
effective and when you learn

133
00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:54.400
all the parameters
of the filter here,

134
00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.640
this turns out to give good
results when you put this in

135
00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:59.470
the context of the union which

136
00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:02.260
is the learning
algorithm will use now.

137
00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:06.124
Let's go back and keep
on building the unit.

138
00:07:06.124 --> 00:07:09.290
In this video, we step
through step-by-step how

139
00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:12.740
the transpose convolution
lets you take a small input,

140
00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:15.875
say two-by-two, and blow
it up into larger output,

141
00:07:15.875 --> 00:07:17.465
say four by four.

142
00:07:17.465 --> 00:07:21.500
This is the key building block
of the unit architecture

143
00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.630
which you get to implement
and play within

144
00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:26.050
this week's programming
exercises well.

145
00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:28.850
Now that you understand
a transpose convolution,

146
00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:30.350
let's take this
building block you now

147
00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.990
have and see how it fits into

148
00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:35.000
the overall unit architecture

149
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.670
that you can use for
semantic segmentation.

150
00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:40.170
Let's go on to the next video.